            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Developing the Glider</strong><br>\nFrom 1900 to 1902, the Wright brothers designed and tested several gliders at Kitty Hawk, North Carolina. They chose this location for its strong winds and soft sands, ideal for their experiments. Their gliders evolved in design, reflecting their growing understanding of aerodynamics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Inventing the Wind Tunnel</strong><br>\nTo better understand the problems with their glider designs, in 1901, the Wright brothers built a wind tunnel. This allowed them to test over 200 types of wings and airframes. Their meticulous testing led to significant advancements in controlling flight through a unique system of ropes and pulleys.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The First Powered Flight</strong><br>\nOn December 17, 1903, they achieved the first controlled, sustained flight of a powered aircraft with Orville piloting their airplane, the Wright Flyer, for 12 seconds over the dunes of Kitty Hawk. This marked a historic moment in human history as the dawn of the age of aviation.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
