          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Patenting their Invention</strong><br>\nUnderstanding the importance of their work, the Wright brothers sought to patent their flying machine. In 1906, they were granted U.S. Patent 821393 for a \"Flying-Machine,\" which detailed their method of flight control, which is still fundamental to all modern aircraft.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Public Demonstrations and Contracts</strong><br>\nThe Wright brothers' public flight demonstrations in 1908 and 1909 amazed spectators and proved their technology worked. Following these successes, they secured contracts in the United States and Europe, training pilots and selling aircrafts, which established their fame and fortune.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Recognition</strong><br>\nThe Wright brothers' contributions to aviation are immeasurable. They overcame skepticism and failure to open the skies to human flight, changing transportation and warfare forever. They received numerous awards and honors during their lifetimes, and their legacy continues to inspire generations of engineers and aviators.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
